@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@using System.Globalization
@using SpaceSails.Client.Rendering
@using SpaceSails.Contracts
@using SpaceSails.Core
@using SpaceSails.Core.Interior
@*
    #251 item 1 · THE SALVAGE OUTCOME CARD, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    Moved out of Map.razor by PURE MOTION — the block between the two fences below is the block that was in
    the page, character for character. Every page member it reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME NAME (the
    fields keep their underscore, the methods come across as delegates), which is what lets the markup move
    without a rewrite and lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as before.

    The `@if` that opens it stays in Map.razor: the page keeps its paint order in one place, and a closed
    surface still renders nothing. IHandleEvent repeats the page's own override (Map.Sim.cs) — without it
    Blazor would put an automatic re-render back on every event in here, which the page turns off because
    the HUD's refresh belongs to OnTick's 200 ms throttle. The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules;
    it exists to give the component Map.razor.css's scope (#251 item 3 moves the rules).

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block used to sit inside `@if (…) { … }`,
    which is a Razor CODE block, and a code block is where its first statements are legal. The braces
    put the moved lines back in the context they were written in; they are outside the fences, so what
    the source guards read is still exactly the lines that were in the page.
*@
@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from Map.razor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <div class="mission-celebration-backdrop" @onclick="() => Dismiss(DismissWreckOutcome)">
            @* ══ #997 wave 5 · THE BEAT CARDS. One comment for all thirteen, on the first of them.
               ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
               These are the surfaces that STOP THE GAME TO TELL YOU SOMETHING: the celebration family
               (a payday, a map, a brief, a reveal, a storyboard), the convergence band (the two mysteries,
               and the four things a captain is told once and never again) and the rescue band. Every one of
               them was the same three things typed again: a scrim that closes it, a stopPropagation to keep
               a click on the card off that scrim, and one worded verb at the foot. Waves 1-4 did this for
               the .view-object family and its borrowers; this is the rest.

               THE THREE PARAMETERS, and they mean the same here as they did there:

                 · Frame="OverlayFrame.Bare" — the way out stays a DIRECT child of the card. That is the
                   relation #735's pinned foot is written against on every one of these roots
                   (`.convergence-card > .convergence-close`, `.expedition-brief-card > button:last-child`,
                   `.rescue-card > .rescue-options`), and Hosted's `display: contents` would put a div
                   between them while the markup went on looking right.
                 · StopClicks="true" — the stopPropagation that was typed on the div, thirteen times.
                 · Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Close" wired to the page's OWN verb — the same method the
                   backdrop behind it already calls, so there is still exactly one road out of each card.

               AND NOT ONE WORD MOVED. Every face below is the exact string its own button carried.
               `Bare` draws the dismiss as the card's last DIRECT child, which is where twelve of the
               thirteen already had it; on the first-ground family (the lesson, the map-just-grew, the tube
               and the air) the button was typed ABOVE its footnote and put back under it by `order: 1`, so
               being last in the DOM now is the same pixel — and it is the reading order the #735 block
               already claims that card has. The one card of the fourteen that could not take a shell is
               named in TheShellOwnsTheBeatCardsTests.TheNamedStragglers, with its reason. ═════════════ *@
            <OverlayShell class="treasure-map-card" StopClicks="true"
                          Frame="OverlayFrame.Bare" Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Close"
                          DismissClass="btn btn-warning btn-sm mt-2" DismissFace="Back to the shuttle"
                          OnClose="() => Dismiss(DismissWreckOutcome)">
                <div class="view-object-title">
                    @(salvageOutcome.ContactEarned ? "📋 FILED" : salvageOutcome.CargoIsHot ? "🏴 STRIPPED" : "📋 FILED")
                </div>
                <div class="tm-caption">@salvageOutcome.Line</div>
                <div class="tm-contents">💰 @salvageOutcome.CreditsNow.ToString("N0") cr</div>
                @* #938 D4 · THE MONEY WAS TAKEN FOR A REASON NOBODY COULD READ. #553 has shaved
                   ComplianceSurcharge.Rate off every filed report since it landed — off the finder's fee AND
                   off the correct-cause bonus — while its five explanatory members had zero consumers
                   anywhere in src. The captain saw a smaller number and was given no clause, which is the
                   one shape this design must not have: "referenced but not described" still needs the
                   reference. It is on the receipt now, where a line item belongs, in the words Core already
                   wrote — the line item cites the clause, the caption is the only summary the game ever
                   offers, and the tooltip is what the office says when a captain asks, seeded off this hull
                   so the same wreck always gives the same non-answer. Nothing here describes the incident.
                   Nothing ever will. *@
                @if (salvageOutcome.SurchargeCr > 0)
                {
                    <div class="tm-bearing" title="@TheClauseNonAnswer">@ComplianceSurcharge.LineItem(salvageOutcome.SurchargeCr)</div>
                    <div class="tm-caption">@ComplianceSurcharge.WhatItIsLine</div>
                }
                @if (salvageOutcome.CargoIsHot)
                {
                    <div class="tm-bearing">🔥 It is in the hold, and it is hot. A collector will know it on sight.</div>
                }
                @* #652 · The half that outlives the payout, WITH A NAME ON IT. This line used to read
                   "Somebody now owes you a straight answer" and there was no somebody: nothing recorded who,
                   nothing persisted, nothing took your call. The assessor is on the ContactLedger now (and
                   therefore in the vault), and the card says whose name is under the finding. *@
                @if (salvageOutcome.ContactEarned && _wreckContact is { Length: > 0 } signedBy)
                {
                    <div class="tm-bearing">@Derelict.ContactLine(signedBy)</div>
                }
            </OverlayShell>
        </div>
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}

@code {
    /// <summary>the page's `string? _wreckContact`</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? _wreckContact { get; set; }
    /// <summary>the page's `Task Dismiss(Action close)`</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<Action, Task> Dismiss { get; set; } = default!;
    /// <summary>the page's `void DismissWreckOutcome()`</summary>
    [Parameter] public Action DismissWreckOutcome { get; set; } = default!;
    /// <summary>the page's `string TheClauseNonAnswer`</summary>
    [Parameter] public string TheClauseNonAnswer { get; set; } = default!;
    /// <summary>bound by the @if that stays in the page</summary>
    [Parameter] public Derelict.SalvageOutcome salvageOutcome { get; set; } = default!;

    // The page's own event dispatch, repeated: no automatic re-render per event.
    Task IHandleEvent.HandleEventAsync(EventCallbackWorkItem callback, object? arg) => callback.InvokeAsync(arg);
}
